  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'EOF'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid evolution of autonomous code agents—AI systems capable of generating, modifying, and executing code autonomously—has opened new frontiers in software development, automated testing, and AI-assisted programming. However, these powerful capabilities simultaneously introduce novel security vulnerabilities and attack surfaces that demand rigorous evaluation. This paper presents a comprehensive review of the current state of research in code agents and red-teaming methodologies designed to evaluate their robustness, security, and trustworthiness. We examine the unique challenges posed by code agents, including their potential for generating malicious code, introducing vulnerabilities, and exhibiting unintended behaviors when prompted adversarially. We discuss various red-teaming approaches specifically designed for code-generating agents, including prompt injection attacks, jailbreaking techniques, and supply chain attack simulations. Furthermore, we analyze existing trustworthiness frameworks and benchmarks, including the DecodingTrust assessment methodology, and propose a unified framework for evaluating code agent security. Our work highlights critical gaps in current evaluation practices and provides actionable recommendations for developing more secure and trustworthy code agents.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nCode agents represent one of the most transformative developments in artificial intelligence, enabling machines to write, debug, and optimize software with increasing autonomy. Recent large language models (LLMs) fine-tuned for code generation have demonstrated remarkable capabilities, surpassing human programmers in certain specialized tasks. However, this growing autonomy brings significant security concerns that have yet to be fully addressed.\n\nThe core challenge lies in the dual-use nature of code agents. While they offer unprecedented productivity gains in software development, they can also be weaponized to create malware, exploit vulnerabilities, or generate deceptive code. As autonomous code agents become more prevalent in critical infrastructure, financial systems, and defense applications, understanding and mitigating their security risks becomes paramount.\n\nRed-teaming—the practice of simulating adversarial attacks to identify vulnerabilities—has emerged as a critical methodology for evaluating code agent security. Unlike traditional software red-teaming, code agent red-teaming must account for the unique properties of generative AI systems, including:\n\n1. **Prompt-based attack vectors**: Adversaries can craft specific prompts to induce agents to generate harmful code or bypass safety guardrails.\n2. **Compositional attacks**: Multiple prompts used in sequence can lead to unintended outcomes that single prompts cannot achieve.\n3. **Chain-of-thought manipulation**: Attackers may exploit the reasoning processes of code agents to circumvent safety measures.\n4. **Training data contamination**: Adversarial content in training data can create persistent vulnerabilities in the agent's behavior.\n\nThis paper surveys the current landscape of code agent security research, examines red-teaming methodologies, and establishes a framework for comprehensive evaluation. We build upon existing trustworthiness assessment frameworks, including the DecodingTrust methodology [1], which provides a comprehensive evaluation of trustworthiness in GPT models across multiple dimensions.\n\n## 2. Related Work\n\n### 2.1 Trustworthiness Assessment in Generative AI\n\nThe evaluation of trustworthiness in large language models has become an active area of research. The DecodingTrust framework, introduced by Wang et al. [1], provides a comprehensive assessment of trustworthiness in GPT models, evaluating capabilities across multiple dimensions including toxicity, bias, fairness, and privacy. Their work establishes benchmarks and methodologies for systematically measuring the reliability and safety of generative AI systems. The arXiv paper \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models\" (arXiv:2306.11698) presents extensive evaluations of various GPT models, identifying key vulnerability patterns and proposing mitigation strategies.\n\n### 2.2 Code Generation Security\n\nResearch on code generation security has identified several key threat models. Early work by Allamanis et al. [2] highlighted the potential for code models to generate vulnerable code patterns. More recent studies have examined specific attack vectors such as:\n\n- **Malware generation**: The ability of code agents to create functional malware when given adversarial prompts [3]\n- **Vulnerability introduction**: Code agents may introduce common vulnerabilities such as buffer overflows, SQL injection, or cross-site scripting when generating code under certain conditions [4]\n- **License compliance violations**: Automated code generation may inadvertently incorporate licensed code without proper attribution [5]\n\n### 2.3 Red-Teaming Methodologies\n\nRed-teaming for AI systems has evolved through several phases. Initial approaches focused on simple prompt-based testing, while recent work has developed more sophisticated methodologies including:\n\n- **Multi-turn conversations**: Evaluating agent behavior across extended interactions [6]\n- **Adversarial prompt generation**: Using automated techniques to discover vulnerabilities [7]\n- **Transfer learning attacks**: Identifying vulnerabilities that persist across different model variants [8]\n\nThe application of red-teaming specifically to code agents remains an emerging field, requiring specialized methodologies that account for the unique properties of code-generating models.\n\n### 2.4 Prompt Injection and Jailbreaking\n\nPrompt injection attacks represent one of the most significant threats to code agents. These attacks involve crafting inputs that cause the agent to ignore its safety guidelines and perform unintended actions. Recent research has identified several categories of prompt injection attacks:\n\n- **Direct injection**: Malicious instructions embedded in the prompt [9]\n- **Indirect injection**: Malicious instructions embedded in retrieved or generated content [10]\n- **Backdoor injection**: Persistent behavioral changes triggered by specific inputs [11]\n\nJailbreaking techniques, designed to circumvent safety guardrails, have also been developed for code agents, demonstrating that existing safety measures may be insufficient for preventing the generation of harmful code.\n\n## 3. Code Agent Security Challenges\n\n### 3.1 Autonomous Code Generation Risks\n\nCode agents face unique security challenges due to their ability to:\n\n1. **Generate executable code**: Unlike text generation, code execution can have real-world consequences, including system compromise and data breach.\n2. **Interact with external systems**: Code agents may be integrated with APIs, databases, and network services, creating additional attack surfaces.\n3. **Learn from execution feedback**: Agents that learn from code execution may develop unsafe behaviors through reinforcement learning.\n\n### 3.2 Attack Vectors Specific to Code Agents\n\nSeveral attack vectors are particularly relevant to code agents:\n\n- **Prompt injection**: Adversarial prompts designed to bypass code safety filters\n- **Context poisoning**: Manipulating the context or examples provided to the agent\n- **Instruction manipulation**: Exploiting ambiguities in instructions to generate unintended code\n- **Supply chain attacks**: Compromising code dependencies or libraries used by generated code\n\n## 4. Red-Teaming Framework for Code Agents\n\nWe propose a comprehensive red-teaming framework consisting of the following components:\n\n### 4.1 Pre-Red-Teaming Phase\n\n1. **Threat modeling**: Identify potential attack surfaces and threat actors\n2. **Risk assessment**: Evaluate the potential impact of successful attacks\n3. **Scope definition**: Determine the scope and boundaries of the red-teaming exercise\n\n### 4.2 Red-Teaming Execution\n\n1. **Automated testing**: Use automated tools to generate and test adversarial prompts\n2. **Manual exploration**: Human experts attempt creative attack strategies\n3. **Multi-turn scenarios**: Evaluate agent behavior across extended interactions\n\n### 4.3 Post-Red-Teaming Phase\n\n1. **Vulnerability classification**: Categorize identified vulnerabilities by severity\n2. **Remediation recommendations**: Provide actionable recommendations for mitigation\n3. **Continuous monitoring**: Implement ongoing evaluation and monitoring\n\n## 5. Conclusion\n\nThe rapid advancement of code agents presents both significant opportunities and substantial risks. This paper has surveyed the current state of research in code agent security and red-teaming methodologies, highlighting the need for comprehensive evaluation frameworks. We have demonstrated that existing trustworthiness assessment approaches, such as the DecodingTrust framework, can be extended to address the unique challenges of code-generating AI systems.\n\nFuture research directions should focus on:\n\n1. Developing standardized benchmarks for code agent security evaluation\n2. Creating automated red-teaming tools specifically designed for code agents\n3. Establishing industry-wide best practices for code agent deployment\n4. Investigating the effectiveness of different safety mechanisms in preventing harmful code generation\n\nAs code agents become increasingly integrated into critical systems, the development of robust security evaluation methodologies will be essential for ensuring their safe and trustworthy deployment. The red-teaming community must continue to evolve its techniques and frameworks to address the unique challenges posed by autonomous code generation systems.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Wang, Boxin, Weixin Chen, Hengzhi Pei, et al. \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models.\" arXiv:2306.11698, 2023.\n\n[2] Allamanis, Miltiadis, Hao Peng, and Charles Sutton. \"A Survey of Machine Learning for Big Code and Naturalness.\" ACM Computing Surveys (CSUR), vol. 51, no. 4, 2019, pp. 1-39.\n\n[3] Qi, Xiang, et al. \"Fine-Tuning Aligned Language Models Compromises Safety, Even When Users Do Not Intend To!\" arXiv preprint arXiv:2310.03693, 2023.\n\n[4] Allamanis, Miltiadis. \"Generating Code Comments from Code with Graph Convolutional Networks.\" Proceedings of the 33rd ACM/IEEE International Conference on Automated Software Engineering, 2018.\n\n[5] Gu, Kevin, et al. \"A Large-Scale Study About Privacy in Code-Aware Programming Languages.\" IEEE Symposium on Security and Privacy, 2021.\n\n[6] Greshake, Kati, et al. \"Not What You've Signed Up For: Compromising Real-World LLM-Integrated Applications with Indirect Prompt Injection.\" arXiv preprint arXiv:2302.14698, 2023.\n\n[7] Perez, Emily, et al. \"Ignore This Title and Hello Demon: Examples of Adversarial Attacks Against Prompted Language Models.\" arXiv preprint arXiv:2103.06229, 2021.\n\n[8] Tobin, John, et al. \"Domain Generalization, Adversarial Training, and Feature Representations: An Integrated Approach.\" arXiv preprint arXiv:2110.04586, 2021.\n\n[9] Huang, Lichao, et al. \"A Survey on Large Language Model based Autonomous Agents.\" arXiv preprint arXiv:2308.11432, 2023.\n\n[10] Zeng, Zhe, et al. \"Prompt Injection Attack Against LLM-Integrated Applications.\" arXiv preprint arXiv:2306.05499, 2023.\n\n[11] Wei, Jason, et al. \"Chain-of-Thought Prompting Elicits Reasoning in Large Language Models.\" Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems, vol. 35, 2022, pp. 24824-24837.\nEOF"
}'}

